  "name": "DACROCYSTOGRAPHY",
  "code": "1073",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"115\" data-end=\"251\"><strong data-start=\"115\" data-end=\"142\">Dacryocystography (DCG)</strong> is a specialized <strong data-start=\"160\" data-end=\"186\">contrast imaging study</strong> of the <strong data-start=\"194\" data-end=\"222\">lacrimal drainage system</strong>, used primarily to evaluate:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"253\" data-end=\"520\">\n<li data-start=\"253\" data-end=\"325\">\n<p data-start=\"255\" data-end=\"325\"><strong data-start=\"255\" data-end=\"283\">Obstruction or narrowing</strong> (stenosis) of the nasolacrimal duct (NLD)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"326\" data-end=\"381\">\n<p data-start=\"328\" data-end=\"381\"><strong data-start=\"328\" data-end=\"361\">Chronic or recurrent epiphora</strong> (excessive tearing)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"382\" data-end=\"452\">\n<p data-start=\"384\" data-end=\"452\"><strong data-start=\"384\" data-end=\"424\">Congenital or acquired abnormalities</strong> of the lacrimal sac or duct</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"453\" data-end=\"520\">\n<p data-start=\"455\" data-end=\"520\">Preoperative planning for <strong data-start=\"481\" data-end=\"512\">dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR)</strong> surgery</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"522\" data-end=\"693\">The test involves injecting a contrast dye into the lacrimal puncta and capturing radiographic images to trace the path of tear drainage from the eyes to the nasal cavity.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Finding</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Possible Interpretation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Complete obstruction of NLD</strong></td>\n<td>Most common cause of chronic tearing, infection (dacryocystitis)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Partial stenosis or narrowing</strong></td>\n<td>May cause intermittent symptoms; often age-related or inflammatory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Delayed contrast drainage</strong></td>\n<td>Suggests functional or anatomical impairment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Contrast reflux back into the canaliculi</strong></td>\n<td>Often indicates distal blockage or high pressure in the system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Diverticula or abnormal sacs</strong></td>\n<td>Suggest prior infections, inflammation, or anatomical anomalies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"522\" data-end=\"693\">&nbsp;</p>",
